    <div class="metrics">
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">now (real)</div><div class="metric-val" id="sim-now">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">projected peak (mcg-equiv)</div><div class="metric-val" id="ss-peak-occ">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">projected trough (mcg-equiv)</div><div class="metric-val" id="ss-trough-occ">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label" data-i18n="ss_peak">ss peak (equiv)</div><div class="metric-val" id="ss-peak">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label" data-i18n="ss_trough">ss trough (equiv)</div><div class="metric-val" id="ss-trough">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label" data-i18n="days_to_ss">days to ss</div><div class="metric-val" id="ss-days">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">daily total</div><div class="metric-val" id="sim-daily">&mdash;</div></div>
      <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label" data-i18n="vs_ceiling">vs ceiling</div><div class="metric-val" id="vs-ceiling">&mdash;</div></div>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:18px;background:var(--bg2);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);padding:16px 18px;font-size:12px;color:var(--text2);line-height:1.85">
      <div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--text3);letter-spacing:.12em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:12px">how to use this simulator</div>

      <p style="margin-bottom:10px">The chart is continuous with the dashboard. Everything left of the <span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">now</span> line is your real logged history, computed exactly the way the dashboard computes it. Use <span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">show history</span> to widen or narrow how far back it reaches &mdash; from 3 days out to your entire log. Everything right of that line is a projection: the drug already on board keeps decaying, and the schedule you&rsquo;ve built above gets added on top of it.</p>

      <p style="margin-bottom:10px"><span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">Building a schedule.</span> Each row is one dose at one clock time. Press a preset (QD, BID, TID, QID) to lay down that many rows with sensible default times, then edit any of them &mdash; type the hour and minute, and type the amount in whatever unit the toggle is set to. Rows do not have to match: 750&nbsp;mcg at 08:00 and 250&nbsp;mcg at 20:00 is a perfectly valid two-row schedule, and the chart will show the asymmetric sawtooth it produces.</p>

      <p style="margin-bottom:10px"><span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">Repeat every N days</span> lets you build patterns longer than a day. Set it to 2 and your rows describe one 48-hour cycle &mdash; that&rsquo;s QOD. The preset button does this for you.</p>

      <p style="margin-bottom:10px"><span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">Ceiling</span> is the upper limit you&rsquo;re trying to stay under &mdash; set it to your current stable peak if you&rsquo;re tapering. The steady-state peak and trough are computed numerically from your actual cycle rather than from a single-dose formula, so uneven splits are handled correctly.</p>

      <p style="margin-bottom:10px"><span style="color:var(--text);font-weight:700">Note on peaks and troughs:</span> buprenorphine&rsquo;s half-life is long (~36&nbsp;hr), so the level stays high between doses. At 24&nbsp;hr dosing with a 36&nbsp;hr half-life the trough sits around 63% of the peak; as you shorten the interval, peak and trough converge. Blood level is modeled so each dose&rsquo;s peak contribution equals the dose amount exactly &mdash; 1,600&nbsp;mcg on board + a 250&nbsp;mcg dose peaks at 1,850&nbsp;mcg. This is why splitting a dose feels smoother than one large dose at the same daily total.</p>

      <p style="margin-bottom:0;padding-top:12px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);color:var(--text3);font-size:11px">This graph is a pharmacokinetic estimate based on a one-compartment model with population-average parameters. Individual response varies. What actually matters is whether your dose silences cravings and keeps withdrawal at bay &mdash; that&rsquo;s between you and your clinician, and no model replaces that feedback loop.</p>
    </div>

