    </div>

    <div class="card" style="font-size:11px;color:var(--text2);line-height:1.85">
      <div class="card-label">disclaimers</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">privacy</span> &nbsp;Your data is stored locally on your device and is not transmitted to a server.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">data</span> &nbsp;Clearing browser data, changing browsers, or changing devices may erase your records unless you create a backup.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">one number to track</span> &nbsp;The main number on the dashboard is a <b>7-day trailing average of dose-equivalent (mcg)</b> — a running estimate of what's on board, built from your own logged doses and a dual-pathway sublingual absorption curve (24/30/36/42 hr half-life, 36 hr by default). It's chosen as the headline number because it's linear: if next week reads roughly double this week, that means roughly double, with no hidden curve to account for. Use it to compare today against last week, not as a lab value.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">receptor occupancy — shown, but not the headline</span> &nbsp;The same dose-equivalent number can be run through a published saturable Emax model (calibrated to PET [¹¹C]carfentanil data, Greenwald 2003) to estimate <b>μ-opioid receptor occupancy</b>. This app still shows that — as a named zone relative to the typical jump-off point, rather than a decimal percentage — because that's the zone actually relevant to a low-dose taper. What it no longer does is present occupancy as a precise number, for the reason below.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">the honest limits of a home estimate</span> &nbsp;What's really happening in your body — actual plasma concentration, actual receptor binding — can only be measured with real lab work or PET imaging. An app built on a dose log can get close, but the gap between "close" and "actual" isn't constant: it starts out meaningful even at higher daily doses, and it grows the lower your dose goes, right through the part of a taper where most of the time is spent. Think of it less as a fixed error margin and more as a downward slope — the deeper into a taper you are, the less any single number on this screen can be trusted at face value, even though the trend it's part of usually still can. That's the honest ceiling on what a home-tracking tool can do, and it's also exactly why the day-to-day comparison, rather than the absolute reading, is the number worth building a habit around.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px;color:var(--blue)"><span style="font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--blue)">the blue shaded areas</span> &nbsp;There are two shaded bands on the chart now, both blue, one darker than the other. The <b>darker band</b> is the statistical jump-off zone — where the discomfort from further reducing the dose is comparable to stopping entirely. Based on further reading, this is placed around a 25–125 mcg/day intake: 0.125 mg/day is treated as the point where reducing further feels about the same as stopping outright, and below roughly 25 mcg/day, intake is effectively indistinguishable from 0 (25 mcg is about the smallest dose change the brain reliably registers). Run through the same steady-state model used elsewhere in the app, that intake range settles to roughly 47–346 mcg-equivalent blood level, which is what's shaded. The <b>lighter band directly above it</b> covers roughly 0.125–0.5 mg/day intake (about 346–1,385 mcg-equivalent blood level) — the range where people who can, often do stop with support (a clinician, stable life circumstances, sometimes detox medications), with real discomfort but not full withdrawal. The top of that lighter band is where stopping outright would typically mean moderate-to-severe withdrawal. For context: people commonly report moderate withdrawal symptoms stopping outright from 500 mcg/day once adjusted to that amount, but at 125 mcg/day, reducing further usually causes about the same degree of discomfort as stopping does. None of this accounts for day-to-day absorption variability, which runs roughly ±34% even at a fixed intake — so a given day's reading can sit noticeably outside either band without anything having changed. A caveat worth being direct about: there's no published study that finely maps withdrawal severity to exact tenths of a milligram in this range — both bands reflect general taper-literature and clinical consensus, not a measured dose-response curve. Treat them as rough zones to aim for, not precise cutoffs.

        <div style="margin-top:12px;padding:10px 12px;background:var(--bg3);border:1px solid var(--border2);border-radius:6px">
          <div style="font-size:9.5px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em;color:var(--text3);margin-bottom:2px">illustrative concept — not measured data</div>
          <div style="font-size:10.5px;color:var(--text2);margin-bottom:8px;line-height:1.5">One way to think about why the zone narrows to a point: as the dose drops, "discomfort from cutting further" and "discomfort from stopping outright" converge, until at some low dose they're about the same. No study has measured this on a numeric scale — the "levels" below are a conceptual illustration of that convergence, not clinical data, and shouldn't be read as validated severity scores.</div>
          <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:10.5px;color:var(--text2)">
            <thead>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border2)">
                <th style="text-align:left;padding:4px 6px 6px 0;color:var(--text3);font-weight:700">stable dose</th>
                <th style="text-align:left;padding:4px 6px 6px;color:var(--text3);font-weight:700">discomfort of dropping a step</th>
                <th style="text-align:left;padding:4px 6px 6px;color:var(--text3);font-weight:700">discomfort of stopping outright</th>
                <th style="text-align:left;padding:4px 0 6px 6px;color:var(--text3);font-weight:700">equivalence</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><td style="padding:5px 6px 5px 0">8 mg</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">very mild</td><td style="padding:5px 6px"><b>severe acute shock</b></td><td style="padding:5px 0 5px 6px">highly unequal</td></tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><td style="padding:5px 6px 5px 0">2 mg</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">highly noticeable</td><td style="padding:5px 6px"><b>severe withdrawal</b></td><td style="padding:5px 0 5px 6px">unequal</td></tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><td style="padding:5px 6px 5px 0">0.5 mg</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">manageable aches/chills</td><td style="padding:5px 6px"><b>moderate withdrawal</b></td><td style="padding:5px 0 5px 6px">narrowing</td></tr>
              <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><td style="padding:5px 6px 5px 0"><b>0.125 mg</b></td><td style="padding:5px 6px">mild anxiety/insomnia</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">mild anxiety/insomnia</td><td style="padding:5px 0 5px 6px"><b>perfect equivalence</b></td></tr>
              <tr><td style="padding:5px 6px 5px 0">0.025 mg</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">baseline tracking</td><td style="padding:5px 6px">baseline tracking</td><td style="padding:5px 0 5px 6px">identical (~0mg)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <div style="font-size:9.5px;color:var(--text3);margin-top:8px;line-height:1.4">The two endpoints (0.125mg and 0.025mg) are the same figures the shaded band above is built from. Everything in between is conceptual scaffolding, not a separate data source.</div>
          <div style="margin-top:10px;padding-top:10px;border-top:1px dashed var(--border2);font-size:10.5px;color:var(--text2);line-height:1.55">
            <b style="color:var(--text)">The takeaway:</b> read this way, <b>0 mg isn't the goal</b>. Stopping from 8mg and stopping from 2mg land in roughly the same rough territory — both well before the curve has narrowed. You can quit at 1mg, but you'll likely feel some withdrawal, less severe than quitting from 2mg but still real. By the time you're stable around 0.125mg, cutting further and stopping outright feel about the same — that's the point, not zero. The goal this framing points to is getting as low as feels sustainable, near that 0.125mg range, and stabilizing there for as long as it takes, rather than treating 0mg itself as the finish line. It's also worth knowing that changes of 25 mcg or less are around where the brain stops registering any meaningful difference — so once decrements get that small, going finer than that isn't expected to feel different, whether the goal is holding steady near 0.125mg or making the final cut from there.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">where tapers end — don't chase zero</span> &nbsp;A taper has a real endpoint. Most people <b>discontinue from around 25–125 mcg/day intake</b> (the "jump-off zone"), often with clinician support. The goal is reaching that zone and stopping, not driving the number to zero. Plan the final jump with your prescriber.</div>
      <div style="margin-bottom:8px"><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">variability &amp; limits</span> &nbsp;Absorption varies dose-to-dose. The Modifier toggle shows a ±1 SD absorption band (within-person CV ≈ 34%) directly on the mcg line. This is a population-average estimate, not a lab measurement — trend and zone-crossings are trustworthy, but the absolute number is not.</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--text3);font-size:10px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.08em">disclaimer</span> &nbsp;This tool is for informational and self-tracking purposes only and is not medical advice. Your decisions are your own, to be made with a licensed physician.</div>
    </div>

